## What I fixed

The code half was already correct and self-verifying (evaluator PASSed intent alignment, touchpoints and product surface). The failure was that CLAUDE.md — the file re-injected as authoritative context every session — carried three false claims about the very gate the diff changed. All three are now corrected, following this repo's own "superseded claim, recorded rather than erased" rule (the Day-183 git-chokepoint payment is the precedent: keep the old census as the reading it was, add a payment paragraph re-deriving numbers from a real run).

| False claim | Correction |
|---|---|
| 2 spawners derived (count_tests_via_cargo, security_audit_command) | Marked stale; payment paragraph names probe_audit_tool and calls security_audit_command a dead symbol as a derived name (the function still exists; it no longer shells) |
| 8 tests reach one; 8 registered as debt | Marked stale; corrected to 0 and 0 with the census pasted verbatim from a real run, never hand-typed |
| #834's option 1 … is the follow-up, not this task | Explicitly marked superseded — that split is what shipped |

Three things I added beyond the literal ask, because they were true and checkable:

• **The derived-name change is the gate's design paying off, not a rename.** The spawn moved into a new function and the derivation followed it with *zero* edits to the gate. Its hand-written sibling `GLOBAL_SETTERS` would have gone stale silently.
• **A limit the payment exposed: limit 3 is a textual match on the spawn, so only the cargo** probe was ever in the gate's population. The four non-cargo probes (`pip-audit`, `safety`, `govulncheck`, `bundle-audit`) were toolchain-dependent in exactly the same way and invisible to it. The seam covers five; the gate counted one.
• **Restated that this was never #832's clobbering defect** — a subcommand probe builds nothing and cannot touch target/debug/yoyo. The harm was toolchain-dependence and vacuous green.

